1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,544
Full autonomy does not mean doing every possible procedure. It means
taking responsibility for noticing the whole situation, stating the
priority, selecting an authorized next step and checking the result
without waiting for prompts.

2
00:00:14,544 --> 00:00:32,760
Before acting, name the patient’s immediate needs, the device concern,
what is unknown and which resource or provider can help. A nurse’s or
advanced practitioner’s device procedure does not transfer to a PCP
just because it appears in a textbook or training scenario.

3
00:00:32,760 --> 00:00:53,136
The Week 13 school objective says not to remove or reposition a device
unless there is an immediate life threat. Apply that instruction to
this learning scenario. It is not a blanket clinical rule for every
device; current device-specific guidance, provider scope and service
direction govern actual practice.

4
00:00:53,136 --> 00:01:10,992
If an immediate threat develops, act only within a current pathway,
your licence and applicable rescue training. This lecture does not
teach tube insertion, catheter flushing, replacement or adjustment.
Use direct instructor supervision for any hands-on device lab.

5
00:01:10,992 --> 00:01:28,656
A 55-year-old woman at home has new abdominal pain and nausea. A
family member reports that her feeding tube shifted while she was
being moved. The patient looks uncomfortable, and there is moisture
near the dressing; no one can confirm when the tube was last used.

6
00:01:28,656 --> 00:01:53,952
This original case follows the school objective without reproducing a
web case stem. Do not use, remove or reposition the displaced tube.
Assess airway, breathing, circulation, pain, abdominal appearance and
any systemic change; obtain the device name, baseline and last-use
history, then follow current guidance and arrange appropriate
escalation.

7
00:01:53,952 --> 00:02:12,888
Report the actual device label or the caregiver’s description, where
it enters or exits, the visible external length if marked, and what
has changed. Observe the site without advancing, rotating, flushing or
testing patency. Do not infer internal placement from appearance.

8
00:02:12,888 --> 00:02:32,856
Abdominal pain plus leakage, increasing tenderness, fever, vomiting,
distension or deteriorating perfusion raises concern and requires
prompt assessment. These findings do not prove peritonitis or sepsis;
use the current abdominal-pain or sepsis pathway and communicate the
uncertainty.

9
00:02:32,856 --> 00:02:52,224
A long-term-care resident has become newly confused and weak. Staff
report that little urine has entered the drainage bag over several
hours; the urine looks darker than usual, and they believe the
catheter may be blocked. The patient is warm, breathing faster than
usual and less interactive.

10
00:02:52,224 --> 00:03:14,880
The device may be part of the story, but the patient’s systemic change
is the priority. Obtain the time course, baseline cognition, observed
output and device history; assess perfusion and other sepsis cues,
follow the current sepsis pathway and request appropriate support. Do
not manipulate the catheter without current authorization and
direction.

11
00:03:14,880 --> 00:03:30,816
Low volume in the collection bag describes drainage, not a confirmed
measure of kidney urine production. Consider the time interval,
baseline, possible leakage or obstruction and other illness; do not
infer one cause from the bag alone.

12
00:03:30,816 --> 00:03:44,016
Urine colour, smell or appearance alone is not a diagnosis of urinary
infection or sepsis. Assess the whole patient and escalate through the
current pathway rather than manipulating the device.

13
00:03:44,016 --> 00:03:59,736
Look for a change from baseline and combine respiratory effort,
perfusion, mental status, measured observations and the patient’s
story. If the patient is deteriorating, communicate that urgency
before discussing device troubleshooting.

14
00:03:59,736 --> 00:04:15,912
A useful handover describes the device in neutral terms: name or type
if known, when it last functioned normally, what output was seen and
what the caregiver observed. Attribute information to its source and
distinguish measured output from estimates.

15
00:04:15,912 --> 00:04:31,800
Also report the patient’s baseline and change, site findings, relevant
medicines or recent feeding, consultation, and the reason the device
was left in place. A device status is part of the handover, not a
replacement for a full patient assessment.

16
00:04:31,800 --> 00:04:46,344
The school weekend asks learners to manage two patients
simultaneously. State which person has the most time-critical threat
and why, assign a named partner to the other patient, and tell both
teams when to report a change.

17
00:04:46,344 --> 00:05:01,560
A plan is not autonomous if the second patient disappears from
attention. Use a timer or spoken check-back, delegate repeat
observations within training and request another crew or clinical
support when available capacity is insufficient.

18
00:05:01,560 --> 00:05:30,624
Return to the patient with the displaced feeding tube. The patient is
worried about missing nutrition, while a family member wants the crew
to put the tube back immediately. First hear the patient's own concern
and assess the current symptoms. The school's patient-centered-care
reading treats the patient as a team member with physical, emotional
and social needs, not as the object of a device repair. Respect the
concern without promising an unsafe or unauthorized intervention.

19
00:05:30,624 --> 00:06:04,056
Explain what you observed, what remains uncertain and what safe
assessment, support or consultation you can offer. Invite the
patient's preferences and ask them to explain the agreed next step in
their own words. A nod or a relative's answer does not establish
understanding. Language, hearing, vision or literacy barriers call for
suitable support; they are not automatic evidence of incapacity.
Patient-centered communication is a clinical action, not a promise
that every requested treatment will be provided.

20
00:06:04,056 --> 00:06:35,616
In our original variation, the patient accepts examination but says
they do not want the tube handled and are unsure about transport.
Clarify each decision rather than documenting refuses everything. Use
the applicable consent and refusal framework, explain the risks and
reasonable alternatives, assess the relevant ability and seek required
consultation. A family majority, a psychiatric history or the fact
that the choice worries you does not by itself remove the patient's
authority.

21
00:06:35,616 --> 00:07:04,800
Continue care that is accepted and authorized while resolving
uncertainty. The reading's potential benefits include trust, error
prevention, self-management and a more feasible plan; they are not
guarantees of compliance or recovery. Check whether the patient can
obtain the agreed help and what support is actually available. A plan
that looks correct on a form can still fail if no one understands it
or the next service has not accepted responsibility.

22
00:07:04,800 --> 00:07:36,672
While you discuss the feeding-tube problem, the second patient's
catheter pain increases. Assign an appropriate colleague to assess
that person and report actual findings, not just to watch them. Retain
an overview of both patients and change priority if systemic
deterioration appears. The nursing text's delegation carryover
emphasizes supervision and evaluation, but the actual PCP licence,
competence and service policy determine what may be assigned. Giving a
task does not erase the need for feedback.

23
00:07:36,672 --> 00:08:08,928
The collaborative-care reading identifies organized communication,
effective protected data sharing, simplified referrals and coordinated
scheduling. Together they reduce gaps between people and services. Ask
for the expertise the problem needs, communicate urgency and confirm a
feasible next step. A referral message sent is not an accepted
appointment, and a portal upload is not an accepted clinical handover.
State who is coordinating the plan and who will act if the patient
changes.

24
00:08:08,928 --> 00:08:42,384
At handover, give the device type and observed status, symptoms and
trends, actions and response, the patient's actual decisions and
remaining uncertainty. Confirm the receiving capability and explicit
acceptance. Use the secure authorized record route, but do not mistake
copying a medication list for reconciling it or assume every team
member needs the entire record. Transparent communication includes
correcting uncertainty and inaccuracy rather than disguising them with
confident wording.

25
00:08:42,384 --> 00:09:13,296
During debrief, compare the intended plan with what both patients and
the team actually understood and did. Did communication uncover a
safety issue? Was the agreed service accessible? Did the second
patient receive a real assessment? Could the receiving clinician
follow the reasoning? Potential convenience and resource savings
should be evaluated, not promised. The school's timed PCR target does
not justify omitting an unresolved clinical concern or an unaccepted
transition.

26
00:09:13,296 --> 00:09:33,480
Before any medication action, verify the patient, medication identity,
strength, indication, route, calculated amount, timing, allergy status
and expiry using the current service monograph and policy. Visually
inspect the product and use the required label if it is removed from
its package.

27
00:09:33,480 --> 00:09:51,360
A mnemonic is not a substitute for an authorized indication, a correct
calculation, consultation or documented administration. If the drug,
patient, dose or route is uncertain, pause and resolve the uncertainty
with the approved resource instead of guessing.

28
00:09:51,360 --> 00:10:07,920
Medication errors and near misses are safety signals. Follow the
service reporting process, document the patient response and notify
the appropriate clinical lead. This lesson does not replace
medication-math gating or hands-on competency assessment.

29
00:10:07,920 --> 00:10:26,376
Nausea may accompany serious conditions, including acute coronary
syndrome, stroke, sepsis, glucose disturbance, gastrointestinal
bleeding, toxic exposure or pregnancy. Assess the patient and likely
cause rather than treating nausea as a stand-alone problem.

30
00:10:26,376 --> 00:10:48,456
BCEHS E07 lists provider-level antiemetic options, including PCP
options. Before administration, follow current monographs, training,
indication, contraindications, dose and route. The school reading on
antiemetics does not authorize any drug outside the learner’s current
licence and service direction.

31
00:10:48,456 --> 00:11:02,856
After symptom treatment, reassess nausea, hydration, airway risk,
mental status and the underlying concern. Improvement in one symptom
does not prove the dangerous cause has been excluded.

32
00:11:02,856 --> 00:11:19,896
Valve stenosis limits forward flow through an opening; regurgitation
allows blood to move backward through an incompletely closed valve.
Either can alter cardiac workload and may contribute to reduced
exercise tolerance, dyspnea, fatigue or syncope.

33
00:11:19,896 --> 00:11:36,984
A murmur is a sound produced by turbulent flow, not a measurement of
how severe the disease is or what is causing today’s symptoms. Ask
about known history and change from baseline; assess current perfusion
and follow the applicable cardiac pathway.

34
00:11:36,984 --> 00:11:56,784
The sympathetic nervous system supports rapid responses such as
increased heart rate and vascular tone; parasympathetic influence
supports restorative functions. Pain, fear, hypoxia or shock may
activate a stress response, but tachycardia alone does not identify
which cause is present.

35
00:11:56,784 --> 00:12:15,072
Fluid balance affects circulation and may be influenced by intake,
losses, renal function and medication. Acid-base balance is linked to
carbon dioxide managed by ventilation and bicarbonate regulated by the
kidneys; respiratory and metabolic disturbances can interact.

36
00:12:15,072 --> 00:12:29,184
Use these mechanisms to ask better questions about vomiting, fluid
intake, medications and baseline disease. They do not allow a
prehospital learner to infer an arterial blood gas or prescribe fluid
from a vague symptom.

37
00:12:29,184 --> 00:13:02,952
Return to the two device patients. Pain, fear, medicines and illness
can all change autonomic activity. Follow the circuit: sympathetic
outflow is thoracolumbar; parasympathetic outflow is craniosacral.
Both use acetylcholine at the ganglion, while the transmitter and
receptor at the organ determine the effect. The adrenal medulla adds a
circulating catecholamine signal. A large pupil or fast pulse is a
finding to interpret, not a specific drug or disease diagnosis.

38
00:13:02,952 --> 00:13:37,296
Connect the mechanism to the complaint. Alpha, beta and muscarinic
effects differ between vessels, heart, airway, glands and smooth
muscle. A medicine can therefore improve one intended symptom while
affecting circulation, alertness or bladder function. Check the actual
product, timing and patient response. For a patient who becomes dizzy
with movement, compare volume, posture, pulse and perfusion rather
than explaining everything as anxiety or assuming the autonomic reflex
worked normally.

39
00:13:37,296 --> 00:14:12,288
Most body water is within cells; extracellular water includes
interstitial fluid and plasma. Sodium and potassium have different
relative distributions, and cell membranes determine movement. Edema
in one compartment does not prove adequate circulating volume. Thirst,
antidiuretic hormone, renal handling and renin-angiotensin-aldosterone
signaling regulate water and electrolytes over different timescales.
These mechanisms help explain the findings but do not supply an
unmeasured electrolyte result.

40
00:14:12,288 --> 00:14:45,864
In the feeding-tube case, ask about actual intake, vomiting and other
losses. In the catheter case, low collected urine may reflect reduced
production, obstruction, displacement or a collection problem. Examine
the patient and observed device status before attributing the number
to kidney failure. A fluid calculation or a normal pressure does not
prove that a bolus is indicated. Follow the current authorized pathway
and evaluate the response, including any sign that breathing or
perfusion worsens.

41
00:14:45,864 --> 00:15:21,624
The bicarbonate and carbon dioxide relationship links lung and kidney
function. Reduced alveolar ventilation can raise carbon dioxide and
favor respiratory acidosis; increased elimination can favor
respiratory alkalosis. Added metabolic acid or bicarbonate loss can
cause metabolic acidosis, while gastric acid loss may favor metabolic
alkalosis. Compensation moves pH toward baseline but does not
establish cure, and two primary problems can coexist. Rate alone does
not measure effective ventilation.

42
00:15:21,624 --> 00:15:55,968
A vomiting patient with deep breathing needs an assessment, not an
automatic acid-base label. Compare illness, intake, glucose as
indicated, renal history, medicines, possible exposures and the
respiratory and perfusion trends. When interpreting a blood-gas
result, preserve arterial or venous sample identity, time and
treatment context; a normal pH can conceal competing processes.
Explain what would increase urgency and request the appropriate
resources while immediate support continues.

43
00:15:55,968 --> 00:16:33,504
The CPSC reading has two tracks: the literal assigned pages concern
drains, while the named Chapter six framework concerns medicine
safety. For our nauseated patient, begin with the current indication
and patient assessment. Check allergies by reaction, relevant
medicines, product and formulation, dose units, route,
contraindications and the required monitoring. Prepare for one patient
at a time and protect the preparation from avoidable interruption. An
unknown syringe or an unclear instruction must be resolved before use.

44
00:16:33,504 --> 00:17:08,088
Use the actual rights and label checks, not a recital that skips
evidence. The textbook lists seven rights; the school may expand the
framework. Recheck at retrieval, preparation and before
administration, label prepared medicine as required and use a
genuinely independent check where required. A tube problem is not
permission to give a drug through that tube. Explain the treatment,
respect the patient's choice, observe delivery and response, then
document what actually happened rather than pre-signing a planned
dose.

45
00:17:08,088 --> 00:17:40,008
High-alert classification concerns the seriousness of harm if a
medicine is used in error. It does not mean every such medicine
produces errors more often. Use layered safeguards and the actual
service requirements; a second person merely agreeing with your
calculation is not an independent check. Several patients, repeated
doses, distraction and fatigue also create risk. A patient asking why
a medicine looks different can reveal a real error, so pause and
investigate.

46
00:17:40,008 --> 00:18:15,696
The source's scheduled-dose timing table belongs to an older acute-
care policy context, not a new rule for delaying emergency care. Check
the actual indication, previous dose and local requirement rather than
inventing a due time. Electronic orders, barcodes and controlled
dispensing can reduce some errors but can also transmit wrong data.
Reconcile the medicine history, check the patient and record actual
administration. If something goes wrong, assess and protect the
patient first, then communicate and report through the appropriate
system.

47
00:18:15,696 --> 00:18:50,256
The literal CPSC pages show compressed-reservoir Hemovac and JP
systems and a curled pigtail example. The photographs explain how a
particular system works, not a universal instruction to open, flush,
replace or remove every device. Identify the actual device, intended
destination and management plan. Shape alone does not establish the
suction arrangement. Support the patient and preserve the observed
system without improvising an invasive repair outside your training
and authority.

48
00:18:50,256 --> 00:19:23,112
A fall in output could reflect less production, obstruction,
displacement or lost suction. Compare symptoms, perfusion, the
insertion site and the visible tubing, and identify multiple drains
separately in handover. The source's routine emptying fractions and
shift interval are planned nursing examples, not reasons to wait when
the patient worsens. Removal requires the actual plan and appropriate
clinician; its Hemovac or JP introduction does not supply a pigtail-
removal procedure.

49
00:19:23,112 --> 00:19:51,936
Autonomy means owning the whole plan, not working alone. Use the
cumulative reader to connect the actual presentation with the relevant
pathway and provider row, consultation and reassessment. The crew is
one microsystem linked to other teams, supplies, policies and
resources. A missing service or connector needs an accepted
alternative and a system report, not an invented destination or a
borrowed medicine from another patient.

50
00:19:51,936 --> 00:20:25,488
Understand system frameworks accurately. IHI's Triple Aim concerns
care experience, population health and per-person cost; its expanded
framework separately recognizes workforce well-being and equity.
Saving money does not automatically create equitable access. In this
case, ask whether the actual patient can understand and reach the
agreed next service, who accepted responsibility and what happens if
the first plan fails. A portal upload or a referral sent is not the
same as that closed loop.

51
00:20:25,488 --> 00:20:56,616
The assigned VSM page is a chapter summary, not a new threshold chart.
It asks you to interpret pressure as an objective cue alongside
baseline, earlier values, pulse and subjective and objective findings.
Check measurement quality and context before treating an isolated
number as a conclusion. Pain, posture, movement, medicines and illness
can all affect a reading. A normal pressure does not exclude an
important perfusion, abdominal or device problem.

52
00:20:56,616 --> 00:21:29,088
Repeat and trend observations while care continues, and recheck sooner
when the condition changes. Do not interpret the book's repeat-
measurement advice as a demand to delay support until a second
pressure is obtained. For each of the two patients, state the main
threat, actual trend, authorized action, expected response and
escalation trigger. Record the decisions and an accepted next plan.
That integrates physiology, safe implementation and systems-based
autonomy.

53
00:21:29,088 --> 00:21:43,968
Blood pressure is one measurement of pressure in the arterial system.
Its significance depends on the patient, baseline, symptoms, technique
and the other circulation findings. A single reading cannot establish
adequate perfusion.

54
00:21:43,968 --> 00:22:01,272
Check cuff size and placement, patient position and measurement
conditions. Compare repeated values with mental status, skin, pulse
quality and clinical response. A stable pressure does not cancel new
confusion, cool skin or a weaker pulse.

55
00:22:01,272 --> 00:22:14,712
A care plan becomes clinically useful when the team implements a
specific authorized action and then checks whether the expected change
occurred. State the measure or observation that will show success
before acting.

56
00:22:14,712 --> 00:22:29,280
If the patient is unchanged or worse, do not keep repeating the same
action because it was in the plan. Reconsider the hypothesis,
equipment, timing and available pathway; request support and document
the new reasoning.

57
00:22:29,280 --> 00:22:55,944
Return to our feeding-tube and catheter patients. You have a plan, but
now distinguish a request from delivery. Did the other patient
actually receive an assessment? Did the intended support reach the
first patient? Implementation needs an appropriate, accepted and
authorized action, the right person and working resources. A task on
the board or a treatment prepared in advance is not proof of care
delivered.

58
00:22:55,944 --> 00:23:23,976
FON Chapter fourteen links implementation, evaluation and
documentation. Use one repeated loop: state the expected response,
act, observe what changed and revise when the result is inadequate or
harmful. Evaluation is not reserved for the end of the call or a later
long-term review. Immediate patient needs, preferences and evidence
can change the plan before a routine task is finished.

59
00:23:23,976 --> 00:23:55,608
Clinical knowledge, critical thinking and judgment answer different
parts of the same task. Knowledge explains the problem; critical
thinking tests the information and alternatives; judgment integrates
the actual patient, urgency and options. Psychomotor skill makes an
authorized procedure reliable, while cognitive and interpersonal
skills support decisions, patient understanding and team coordination.
A technically smooth action is still wrong if it was not indicated or
accepted.

60
00:23:55,608 --> 00:24:27,144
Prioritize the threat and allocate personnel, equipment, time and
information deliberately. Patient-centered care, evidence,
improvement, safety, teamwork and informatics are linked quality
themes, not six disconnected definitions. Do not turn a nursing
illustration into a new treatment bundle. The book's leg-position,
oxygen, fluid or chronic-medicine examples require the actual
presentation and current provider guidance, not automatic imitation.

61
00:24:27,144 --> 00:25:00,984
Direct or indirect describes how an action reaches the patient.
Independent, dependent or interdependent describes the authority or
collaboration behind it. A patient explanation may be direct and
within authorized independent practice; obtaining a receiving plan may
be indirect and collaborative. Neither classification creates a
licence. Similarly, actual, potential and collaborative problems can
overlap; a small existing problem does not outrank a credible imminent
threat simply because it is actual.

62
00:25:00,984 --> 00:25:29,016
Before assigning the second patient, check the five delegation
questions: is this the right task and circumstance, is the person
competent and authorized, are the instructions clear, and how will
supervision and report-back occur? Maintain an overview and an
escalation trigger. The textbook's nursing role examples are not
universal BC PCP rules, and giving a task does not erase either
person's responsibility for their own work.

63
00:25:29,016 --> 00:25:58,848
Suppose one patient reports less discomfort but becomes harder to
engage while breathing grows shallow. Do not declare the goal met from
the pain score alone. Assess the actual change and immediate breathing
and perfusion needs, then follow the relevant pathway and escalation.
Evaluation combines intended benefit, adverse effects and remaining
threats. A better monitor number or a completed action does not
establish overall recovery.

64
00:25:58,848 --> 00:26:30,624
For a met goal, ask what care still has an indication. For a partly
met or unmet goal, verify delivery, reconsider the explanation and
revise with appropriate consultation. Stopping an unnecessary
intervention is different from abandoning the patient. Document the
reason, the person's decision, what support continues and who accepts
the next plan. Do not independently discontinue prescribed long-term
treatment because one teaching example says terminate.

65
00:26:30,624 --> 00:27:00,048
Use the school's five-minute PCR rehearsal to explain Donabedian. A
form and trained crew are structure. An accurate account of both
patients, real actions and accepted handover is process. Safety,
understanding and continuing care are outcomes to examine. Finishing
faster is not proof of improvement if the note omits the quieter
patient or invents a normal finding. Measure omissions and errors as
well as time.

66
00:27:00,048 --> 00:27:29,760
Quality assurance asks whether standards and supporting resources are
in place; improvement tests ways to make care better. Include patient,
team and data perspectives. Patient surveys and nursing-sensitive
indicators can reveal gaps, but satisfaction is not a clinical safety
certificate and a complication rate alone does not identify its cause.
Use clear definitions and comparable populations, not a convenient
database name or an isolated score.

67
00:27:29,760 --> 00:28:00,408
For each patient, record relevant baseline and changes, actual support
and response, the source of information, consultation, specific
choices and the accepted next plan. Explain how you know the patient
understood, rather than writing understands because a leaflet was
supplied. Record the actual intervention, not a request or a prepared
dose. Chart promptly when urgent care permits, using the approved
process for corrections or late entries.

68
00:28:00,408 --> 00:28:32,280
Objectivity does not mean hiding a clinically important refusal, delay
or communication failure. Describe the concern, information exchanged,
observed facts and resulting decision without disparaging labels.
Immediate danger takes priority over perfect prose; a breathing
problem after a medicine cannot wait for a complete paragraph. A
truthful record supports continuity and accountability but cannot
guarantee a legal outcome or substitute for the care itself.

69
00:28:32,280 --> 00:29:06,624
Source-oriented records group information by form or professional: an
admission reference, repeated observations on a flowsheet and a
narrative explaining significant events. SOAP instead groups
subjective report, objective findings, assessment and plan. SOAPIER
adds the actual intervention, evaluation and revision. Separate the
interpretation from a confirmed diagnosis, and a plan from what was
actually delivered. Use the real required PCR format rather than
creating duplicate forms for every acronym.

70
00:29:06,624 --> 00:29:37,488
PIE links problem, intervention and evaluation. F-DAR links a focus
with data, action and response. Charting by exception requires an
approved system and actually assessed norms; it never makes an
unexamined system normal. Case-management records track a complex plan
across services and time. Whatever the format, another clinician
should be able to identify the current concern, what happened, what
changed and who owns the unresolved next step.

71
00:29:37,488 --> 00:30:04,656
Return to the feeding-tube patient who is vomiting. First identify
immediate airway, breathing and perfusion needs, then examine the
symptom and its context. A tube does not explain every episode, and an
antiemetic does not confirm tube position or fix an obstruction. Ask
what changed from baseline, what preceded the vomiting, and which
findings require urgent escalation rather than another routine task.

72
00:30:04,656 --> 00:30:34,848
The brainstem coordinates inputs from the gut, circulating chemicals,
vestibular pathways and higher centers. Receptors explain treatment
targets, not the final diagnosis. Antacids neutralize existing acid;
they are not all acid-production suppressors and do not treat every
cause of nausea. If the patient vomits less but remains distended,
poorly perfused or less alert, symptom control has not established
recovery.

73
00:30:34,848 --> 00:31:06,720
Map the six families rather than learning one interchangeable
antiemetic list. Chlorpromazine and prochlorperazine illustrate
dopamine-related phenothiazine effects. Promethazine has stronger
antihistamine and anticholinergic characteristics with much weaker
dopamine antagonism. Hydroxyzine and meclizine illustrate H one
antihistamines; scopolamine illustrates antimuscarinic therapy. A
chemical family, receptor effect and clinical indication are three
different labels.

74
00:31:06,720 --> 00:31:36,072
Ondansetron and granisetron target serotonin receptors; aprepitant and
rolapitant target the substance P pathway. Many of the reading
examples are planned chemotherapy or postoperative prevention, not
rescue orders for this device encounter. Dronabinol acts at
cannabinoid receptors in a selected chemotherapy setting. These
distinctions help you understand a medicine history without making all
eleven table prototypes authorized PCP treatments.

75
00:31:36,072 --> 00:32:04,104
The assigned table contains errors and compressed regimens. Compro is
a prochlorperazine product, not a chlorpromazine brand. The dronabinol
chemotherapy comparison loses the per-square-meter unit, and the
scopolamine line confuses patch content with delivery. A number
without the correct drug, formulation, units and indication is not a
usable dose. Never repair it by guessing or copying a neighboring row.

76
00:32:04,104 --> 00:32:34,368
Aprepitant illustrates why checking matters more than a quick
correction. Weight-based suspension labeling can include adults unable
to swallow capsules, so do not declare every adult weight-based entry
false. It still needs the right product, population, maximum and
later-day regimen. Actual intravenous aprepitant also exists.
Likewise, a granisetron chemotherapy schedule is not an automatic
repeat-dose rule for every vomiting patient.

77
00:32:34,368 --> 00:33:05,232
Suppose the patient is now quieter but harder to engage after a recent
medicine. Reassess breathing, consciousness and perfusion instead of
recording effective because vomiting stopped. Alcohol, opioids and
other depressants can add risk. Watch for hypotension, falls and
abnormal movements. An acute dystonic reaction is not simply anxiety,
and fever with marked rigidity and altered awareness requires urgent
attention rather than a behavioral label.

78
00:33:05,232 --> 00:33:35,040
Anticholinergic effects are particularly relevant to our catheter
patient: retention, constipation, blurred vision and confusion may
complicate the presentation. A scopolamine patch may be missed in the
medication history; handling can transfer medicine to the eye, and
reduced sweating can contribute to overheating. A possible medicine
effect does not prove causation or exclude another emergency. Keep the
actual findings and uncertainty visible.

79
00:33:35,040 --> 00:34:03,744
Do not flatten all class warnings into one rule. Promethazine
injection is contraindicated below age two and still requires caution
in older children; it can also cause severe tissue injury. Hydroxyzine
injection has its own intramuscular-only restriction and early-
pregnancy and prolonged-QT contraindications. These selected label
comparisons correct the textbook; they do not authorize you to
administer these products or improvise a route.

80
00:34:03,744 --> 00:34:38,280
For any authorized medicine, verify the current indication, patient
factors, allergies, prior doses, co-medications and exact product
instructions. Ondansetron labeling adds QT and serotonergic concerns;
nursing antipsychotic mortality warnings have their own context.
Access to a tube or vein does not establish compatibility or
authority. Use the actual school and service monograph, including the
already-taught dimenhydrinate pathway, not the broad chapter as a new
formulary.

81
00:34:38,280 --> 00:35:10,488
Evaluate symptom relief together with consciousness, airway
protection, breathing, perfusion and relevant adverse effects.
Persistent symptoms require a new look at the cause, not automatic
dose stacking. Tailor education and comfort measures: oral fluids or
gum are not safe for every swallowing problem, and a fluid-restricted
patient does not automatically need extra intake. Explain relevant
driving, alcohol and warning-sign advice, then check real
understanding.

82
00:35:10,488 --> 00:35:38,952
The final assigned page also opens diarrhea assessment. Duration,
blood, infection concerns, fluid loss and perfusion matter before
deciding whether suppressing stool is appropriate. Apply the same
patient-first logic to both device patients and preserve the actual
assessment, choices, administered treatment, response and accepted
handover in the PCR. A textbook mechanism is useful only when it
supports that complete clinical chain.

83
00:35:38,952 --> 00:36:06,072
Our catheter patient needs physical help, while the other patient
needs a slower explanation. Neither fact tells you they cannot decide.
Autonomy, independence and agency are different: a person can need
practical assistance and still direct their own care. Ask what each
person wants, what support helps and which decision is being made now.
Representation starts with their objectives, not with your preferred
answer.

84
00:36:06,072 --> 00:36:36,720
Empowerment develops self-reliance, participation, dignity and
belonging. Build on a real strength, explain an option and agree a
manageable next step. Practical assistance is not automatically
harmful enabling; it may make participation possible. Avoid the
rescuer loop in which you take over, the person becomes quieter, and
that silence is mistaken for inability. A diagnosis or frequent
attendance does not determine worth or comprehension.

85
00:36:36,720 --> 00:37:07,632
Use the six trauma-informed practices in the reading: safety, trust,
chosen support, partnership, strengths and cultural or gender
responsiveness. Introduce the next action before touching a device,
explain what is available and invite questions. Include supporters
appropriately without automatically disclosing private information or
transferring the decision. Transparency matters most when the plan has
limits or the patient has had a frightening prior experience.

86
00:37:07,632 --> 00:37:37,128
Sharing power does not require unsafe treatment or abandoning urgent
care. Explain real options and boundaries, assess the specific concern
and obtain appropriate help when the lawful route is uncertain. Pain,
distress, disability or disagreement alone does not authorize taking
over. Preserve participation as far as possible and use the actual
consent and service framework, not the textbook's broad nursing
examples as an override rule.

87
00:37:37,128 --> 00:38:01,944
Self-advocacy makes the person's questions and needs heard; it does
not require unaided speech. Ask what is unclear, offer a usable
explanation and help formulate one question for the next clinician.
Model how to clarify an answer, then make room for the person to try.
Check what they actually understood instead of treating a leaflet or a
family member's agreement as proof.

88
00:38:01,944 --> 00:38:32,184
The six-step judgment model also applies to advocacy. Notice the
unanswered concern, analyze its context, identify the priority,
generate an appropriate solution, carry it out and evaluate the
result. For our quieter patient, that may mean arranging communication
support while a colleague maintains the other patient's clinical plan.
Verify both the completed explanation and the ongoing assessment;
advocacy and safety are not competing tasks.

89
00:38:32,184 --> 00:39:01,032
Use the five advocacy actions to check this encounter. Keep each
person informed, mediate the current disagreement respectfully,
safeguard against error or privacy loss, show equal dignity and
address an actual access barrier. Set a realistic goal and preserve
the patient's voice; agreement between professionals is not enough.
Escalate a safety concern through the proper route instead of hiding
it to avoid tension.

90
00:39:01,032 --> 00:39:31,368
Match the repair to the barrier. Missing equipment needs a resource
plan; inaccessible information needs communication support; hierarchy-
related fear needs a supported reporting path. In the PCR, preserve
choices, supports, real findings and actions, unresolved concerns and
accepted handover for each patient. During debrief choose one
observable improvement and how to test it next time, not simply that
the scene felt calmer or the record was faster.

91
00:39:31,368 --> 00:39:57,936
A valve lesion is another reason a device patient may deteriorate
without the visible tube being the main cause. Begin with the valve as
a one-way gate. Stenosis restricts forward passage; regurgitation
allows backward leakage. Which chamber and which phase are involved
determine the loading pattern. Total blood ejected from a ventricle is
not always the amount delivered forward to the organs.

92
00:39:57,936 --> 00:40:26,208
Use the normal cycle to orient a murmur. Aortic stenosis obstructs
systolic outflow, while mitral regurgitation leaks back during
systole. Mitral stenosis obstructs diastolic filling, while aortic
regurgitation leaks back during diastole. These mechanisms guide
interpretation, not a field anatomy diagnosis. Assess breathlessness,
perfusion, rhythm and the change from usual function alongside the
sound.

93
00:40:26,208 --> 00:40:56,760
The assigned chapter describes several routes to structural damage.
Calcification stiffens tissue; aortic cusps and the mitral annulus
have different consequences. A prolapsing mitral leaflet can lose
effective closure and cause regurgitation. Rheumatic immune injury can
later thicken, fuse and scar the valve and chordae. Do not reverse the
prolapse-to-leak explanation or assume rheumatic disease is the only
possible cause of mitral stenosis.

94
00:40:56,760 --> 00:41:26,376
A chronic lesion may be compensated until another illness increases
demand or reduces reserve. Acute severe leakage may produce a rapid
change without that adaptation. Ask about known valve disease, repair
or replacement, baseline exercise tolerance and the current change
when relevant. Then choose the appropriate patient-level pathway. A
familiar murmur or a past diagnosis should not distract from a new
breathing or perfusion problem.

95
00:41:26,376 --> 00:41:56,016
Infective vegetations can damage the valve or nearby tissue and send
infected or obstructing material elsewhere. Presentation is variable;
no fever or no classic peripheral sign cannot exclude the condition.
Sterile vegetations are different but not necessarily harmless.
Hypercoagulable thrombotic lesions can embolize, and lupus-related
lesions involve immune injury. A source photograph is not a diagnosis
you can make from one symptom.

96
00:41:56,016 --> 00:42:28,440
Carcinoid mediator injury provides another mechanism, often affecting
right-sided valves. For our weekly encounter, use this pathology to
widen a thoughtful history, not to invent a rare diagnosis. A new
focal deficit, sepsis pattern or acute congestion needs its relevant
urgent pathway. Preserve observed findings, uncertainty, authorized
support and accepted handover. Neither a loud murmur nor a quiet
examination measures the whole risk.

97
00:42:28,440 --> 00:42:44,640
The saved school plan names a five-minute PCR completion target for
the weekend practice. Treat this as a school exercise standard, not a
universal statutory deadline. Use the actual rubric and service PCR
system for required fields.

98
00:42:44,640 --> 00:43:04,752
A concise record can still be complete: timeline, patient baseline,
device condition, objective findings, actions and response, medication
verification, consultation, capacity or consent discussion, and
destination handover. Never fabricate a missing detail to meet the
timer.

99
00:43:04,752 --> 00:43:30,816
The school practises SBAR or MIST. Keep that exercise requirement
distinct from current BCEHS A zero three: ATMIST AMBO for transfer of
care, and SBAR for consultation. In either setting, communicate the
findings, device uncertainty, actions and response, then confirm who
accepted the next responsibility. A transmitted report alone is not a
completed transfer.

100
00:43:30,816 --> 00:43:43,920
The Week 13 school plan asks the learner to lead all seven debrief
questions. Start with the patient’s main problem, then identify the
cue that changed your thinking and the competing explanation you
considered.

101
00:43:43,920 --> 00:44:00,576
Next examine the priority, authorized actions and observed response.
Finish by naming one specific practice target and how the next run
will test it. A debrief should improve the next attempt, not assign
blame or merely list the steps.

102
00:44:00,576 --> 00:44:15,984
A device can be displaced, blocked or malfunctioning while the
patient’s most urgent problem is systemic. Keep the device where it
is, avoid unsupported manipulation and use the appropriate pathway to
address the patient’s condition.

103
00:44:15,984 --> 00:44:32,016
Full autonomy is visible in the complete loop: assess, prioritize, act
within scope, evaluate and communicate. This is the standard you will
practise in the device lab, two-patient scenario and timed
documentation exercise.
